Subject: an htree count is bounded by its limit before it sizes a checksum

ext4_dir_dx_csum_verify() checks that the index block's limit fits inside
the block -- and then hands ext4_dir_dx_checksum() the COUNT, which is
what sizes the region crc32c reads. Both come off the medium, and nothing
required count <= limit.

    ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow
      crc32 <- ext4_crc32c <- ext4_dir_dx_checksum
      <- ext4_dir_dx_csum_verify <- ext4_dir_dx_find_entry
      <- ext4_dir_find_entry

which is a lookup: opening a file by name in an indexed directory.

A directory claiming more entries than its own index block can hold is
corrupt, so verify treats it as a checksum failure -- which is exactly
the question the caller asked. The writer gets the same bound for the
other reason: stamping a checksum computed over bytes outside the block
would be worse than not stamping one.

Found by fuzzing, read-only pass, in the campaign after a corpus merge.

Fixture Tests/fixtures/hostile/0009-an-htree-count-past-its-limit.img.gz.

--- a/src/ext4_dir_idx.c
+++ b/src/ext4_dir_idx.c
@@ -300,6 +300,22 @@
 			/* There is no space to hold the checksum */
 			return true;
 		}
+		/*
+		 * limit is checked against the block above; count was not, and
+		 * count is what sizes the region ext4_dir_dx_checksum reads.
+		 * Both come off the medium, and a count past the limit sends
+		 * crc32c beyond the block:
+		 *
+		 *   AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow
+		 *     crc32 <- ext4_crc32c <- ext4_dir_dx_checksum
+		 *     <- ext4_dir_dx_csum_verify <- ext4_dir_dx_find_entry
+		 *
+		 * A directory that claims more entries than its own index
+		 * block can hold is corrupt, so this is a checksum failure --
+		 * which is exactly what the caller is asking about.
+		 */
+		if (cnt < 0 || cnt > limit)
+			return false;
 		t = (void *)(((struct ext4_dir_idx_entry *)climit) + limit);
 
 		uint32_t c;
@@ -334,6 +350,13 @@
 			return;
 		}
 
+		/* The writer's half of the same bound: count sizes the region
+		 * being checksummed, and stamping a checksum computed over
+		 * bytes outside the block would be worse than not stamping
+		 * one. See the note in ext4_dir_dx_csum_verify. */
+		if (count < 0 || count > limit)
+			return;
+
 		t = (void *)(((struct ext4_dir_idx_entry *)climit) + limit);
 		t->checksum = to_le32(ext4_dir_dx_checksum(inode_ref, dirent,
 					coff, count, t));
